There are two interpreters.
The original (no_stack.c) does not use a stack to keep track of loops. Instead,
it dynamically computes where to jump to every single time, which makes it very
slow, but decreases memory usage. The original also does not read the entire
file into memory before interpreting it, which also slows it down but reduces
memory footprint.

The new version (stack.c) uses a stack to keep track of loops; it only
dynamically computes the next position when at an open loop and a jump must be
made to skip the loop. The new version also reads the entire file into memory
before interpreting it, which speeds up runtime but increases memory footprint.

When it comes to pure speed, the original is very slow. The new version
may have comperable performance to the compiler at zero optimization with
wrapping behavior. I have observed a 10 second difference in favour of the new
interpreter when compared to the compiler on the mandelbrot fractal
viewer on my T480. However, on my desktop, the compiler outperforms the
interpreter.
